    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. When stacking pallets on high-density racking systems, it's crucial to maintain a low center of gravity to ensure stability. The center of gravity should be as low as possible, ideally at the base of the stack. This can be achieved by placing heavier items at the bottom and lighter items on top.\n\nFor interlocking patterns, consider using a staggered or offset pattern. This means that each row of pallets should be offset from the one below it, similar to the pattern used in brickwork. This helps to distribute the weight evenly and prevents the entire stack from tipping over if one pallet shifts or is removed.\n\nTo calculate the center of gravity, you can use the following formula:\n\nCenter of Gravity (CG) = (Σ (Weight x Distance)) / Total Weight\n\nWhere:\n- Weight is the weight of each individual pallet or load\n- Distance is the distance of each pallet or load from a reference point (usually the base of the stack)\n- Total Weight is the sum of the weights of all pallets or loads in the stack\n\nBy calculating the center of gravity, you can determine the optimal placement of pallets to ensure stability. Additionally, it's important to regularly inspect the racking system and pallets for any signs of damage",
